  "title": "Domain and Range",
  "body": " Domain and Range   This Lesson contains several geometry problems that lend themselves to a discussion of domain and range. Working through these problems will provide practice with algebraic skills.  If students struggle with the first Activity, you might work through it with them, and perhaps assign Activity 2 as homework. Note that the function in Activity 2 is a rational function, and the function in Activity 3 is a square root.  GOALS   Find the domain and range of a function from its graph  Find the domain and range of a function from its formula  Find a suitable domain for a function used as a model  Find the range of a function on a given domain   Area of a Triangle  Consider several different lines that pass through the point and form right triangles in the first quadrant, as shown below. The shape of the triangle depends on the slope of the line, . Let and stand for the - and -intercepts of the line, respectively.  line in first quadrant through  Draw a few different lines that pass through for students.   Use the point-slope equation of the line, , to find and in terms of .  Students may have trouble finding and in terms of . Remind them that the intercepts are points with coordinates and .  Find the area of the triangle as a function, , of the slope of the line.  What is the domain of this function?  Graph your function for values of between and .  Make a sketch of your graph.  For what value of does the triangle have the smallest area? Sketch that triangle, and compute its area  What happens to the area of the triangle as decreases through negative values? What is the range of the function ?    Another Triangle  A right triangle is formed in the first quadrant as follows: Two of the sides are formed by the - and -axes, the third side is on a line passing through the point . (Note that this is the same picture as in Activity 1.)  Note that the setting for this Activity is the same as the previous one, but using a different independent variable.   Let be the -intercept of the hypotenuse of the triangle. Express the slope of the hypotenuse in terms of .  Let be the -intercept of the third side. Express in terms of . (Use your expression for the slope.  Express the area of the triangle as a function of , .  What is the domain of your function? (What values of result in a triangle?)  In each Activity, encourage students to use both algebraic and graphical methods to find the domain and range of the function.  Graph your function on the domain . Make a sketch of your graph.  What is the area of the triangle if ?  What value(s) of result in a triangle of area 7.2 square units?  What value of results in the triangle with the smallest area?  What is the area of that triangle?  Describe what happens to the area of the triangle as increases from 2 to larger values. What is the range of the function ?    Yet Another Triangle  The hypotenuse of a right triangle is 12 centimeters long.   If the length of one leg of the triangle is , what is the length of the other leg?  Express the area of the triangle as a function of , .  What is the domain of your function?  Graph your function on its domain.  What is the area of the triangle if the shortest side is inches long?  For what value of does the triangle have the largest area?  What is its area?  What is the range of the function ?    Checkpoint   The same triangles are described in Activities 1 and 2. What is different about the area functions you wrote in each Activity?  In Activity 1, describe the motion of the hypotenuse as increases over its domain.  In Activity 2, describe the motion of the hypotenuse as increases over its domain.  In Activity 3, how can you find the domain of the function from its formula?   Questions for Writing or Discussion   In Activity 1, Delbert came up with the equation . How did he get this?  Francine says that the expression can be simplified to . Is she correct? Why or why not?  Do the largest and smallest values in the domain of a function result in the largest and smallest values of the range? Give an example.  Which of the eight basic functions include all real numbers in their range?   Concept Questions   Which function changes concavity on its domain?        Which function includes all real numbers in its range?        In a function, how many domain elements can be paired with one range element?   One  Two  None  Infinitely many   The domain of the function is . What is the domain of the function ?         "
